Today I learned about PMOS because recently the lecture had been covering concepts related to NMOS, and today we shifted from NMOS to PMOS. PMOS is largely the opposite of NMOS. In many equations and concepts, wherever there is an N, you can replace it with a P, and wherever there is a P, you can replace it with an N. In PMOS, we apply a negative voltage to the gate instead of a positive one. If we make the gate negative with respect to the source, and the drain is also at a lower potential than the source, the PMOS will operate in saturation if the required saturation condition is satisfied.

After that, we moved on to the concept of finding the input and output impedance of a PMOS. Basically, I don't think I need to explain the entire procedure because it is the same method that we followed for finding the input and output impedance of an NMOS. First, we set all the independent sources to zero. Then we apply a test voltage at the input or output, depending on what impedance we want to find. Finally, we calculate the impedance using (R = \frac{V}{I}).

Then I learned about AC ground. Initially, I thought that ground and AC ground were the same thing, but later I realized that they are actually different concepts. An AC ground is a node where the AC or small-signal voltage does not change with time, even though the DC voltage at that node may not be zero. So, for AC signals, that node behaves like ground.

Next, we studied the diode-connected MOSFET. In a diode-connected MOSFET, we simply short the gate and the drain together. Earlier I mistakenly said source and gate, but it is actually the drain and the gate that are connected. We also derived the equation for the input impedance of the diode-connected MOSFET.

Then we went through the T-model of the common-source configuration. I don't think I need to explain it in detail because we had already studied a similar T-model for BJTs. The procedure and the overall idea are quite similar. We also discussed common-source biasing techniques and self-biasing, which again were conceptually similar to what we had learned in BJTs.

One of the most interesting topics I learned today was how an antenna works. Initially, I wondered how a simple piece of wire could transmit information through space or air. Later I understood that an antenna is not some unique or highly complex device. It is simply a conductor, although antennas can have different shapes depending on the application, such as a straight wire, a helical antenna, or other geometries.

The basic idea is that when an alternating current flows through the antenna, the electric and magnetic fields around it keep changing. These changing fields generate electromagnetic waves, which propagate through space. Electromagnetic waves travel at the speed of light in a vacuum, which is much faster than sound waves. Sound is a mechanical wave that requires a material medium to travel, whereas electromagnetic waves do not require any medium.

This also helped me understand why sunlight reaches the Earth at the speed of light. Sunlight itself is an electromagnetic wave. Similarly, almost all modern communication systems, such as radio, television, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, satellite communication, and mobile networks, use electromagnetic waves to transmit information from one place to another.
